Carbon Copia is less a band and more an archival collective, operating from the sub-level water-filtration plants of Arkology 7, a sprawling, moss-covered complex built on what was once the delta of the Rio Negro. Their sonic excavations are led by "Cipher," a fluidly gendered sound-weaver whose modified TR-909, adorned with bioluminescent fungi, acts as a primary interface for conjuring fractured beats. "Veridia," the lead vocalist and lyricist, fronts the group; her voice, digitally aged and then resampled, oscillates between spectral lament and defiant chant, often singing in a future-patinaed Portuguese infused with coded whispers. The third member, "Lens," is the collective's visual architect, projecting corrupted holographic flora and spectral data streams during their performances, often incorporating live-processed feeds of the decaying environment outside the Arkology. Carbon Copia interprets the "data-ghosts" of the infamous corrupted cassette, coaxing melodies from failing systems and building rhythm from digital decay, transforming their performances into spiritual seances channeling the forgotten energies of a lost time.

